This pickleball paddle was designed to comply with the 2026 USA Pickleball Official Rulebook while maintaining durability for real gameplay. The overall dimensions do not exceed the maximum allowable combined length and width of 24 inches, and the paddle face follows a standard rectangular geometry to ensure consistent performance and control.

The paddle features a solid-core 3D printed structure with a 15% honeycomb infill pattern. This infill was selected to reduce overall weight while still maintaining sufficient structural integrity and impact resistance. The honeycomb geometry distributes stress efficiently and provides a balance between stiffness and energy absorption during ball contact.

For the paddle face, I used carbon fiber–reinforced ABS, which offers increased stiffness, durability, and resistance to repeated impacts. The handle core and structural support components were printed in PLA for rigidity and dimensional accuracy. The face thickness was increased slightly within legal limits to prevent cracking during play.

Additionally, fillets and rounded edges were incorporated to reduce stress concentrations and improve durability. The design was validated through simulated impacts and short gameplay testing to confirm it can withstand typical use without fracture or delamination.


The handle was designed as a multi-material system to optimize both structural integrity and user comfort. The internal handle core was printed in PLA, providing a rigid and stable connection between the paddle and the user’s hand. To enhance grip and reduce vibration, a TPU (thermoplastic polyurethane) overgrip was added.

TPU was selected for its flexibility, shock absorption, and high friction coefficient. The grip features a lattice-textured pattern that slightly compresses under pressure, improving ergonomics and reducing hand fatigue during extended gameplay. This also minimizes vibration transfer during ball impact, improving overall control.

The paddle includes an interchangeable edge guard, designed as a modular snap-fit component that attaches securely around the paddle perimeter. This allows for easy replacement when wear occurs due to ground contact. The edge guard was printed in PLA for rigidity and ease of manufacturing; however, the modular design allows future iterations to use PETG or TPU for improved impact resistance. This approach extends paddle lifespan and enables customization based on playing conditions.


This paddle was intentionally designed within the heavy weight category (>8.3 oz), targeting approximately 8.6–8.8 oz. The goal of this design was to prioritize power and shot stability over maximum maneuverability. A heavier paddle increases swing momentum, allowing for stronger drives, deeper shots, and more powerful serves with less required swing speed.

To achieve this, carbon fiber–reinforced ABS was used in the paddle face to add both strength and mass, while internal infill distribution was adjusted to concentrate weight toward the paddle head. This resulted in a slightly head-heavy balance, which further enhances power generation by increasing swing inertia.

To offset the reduced maneuverability typically associated with heavier paddles, the handle was kept relatively lightweight using a PLA core with a TPU grip, preserving a degree of control and responsiveness. This balance supports an aggressive, power-focused style of play, particularly effective for baseline shots and driving rallies, while still maintaining sufficient control for net play.


Since there was internal connection, the heatsets designed to connect between the handle and paddle were not used. 

